WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.740
Steve Kaplan: New skills start to appear in
Melissa McCarthy’s case [SPY movie].

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:23.840
Courage, boldness, everything that she wasn’t
in the Normal World, back in her basement

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.770
office at the CIA, that vermin-ridden basement
office.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:41.110
So you have the Reaction and then there’s
a part of the movie that we call Connections.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:52.600
Connections is the part of the movie when
your characters start to find unexpected allies

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.140
and (sometimes unexpected) enemies.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:06.390
In TROPIC THUNDER, the two main actors, Robert
Downey, Jr. who is playing an Australian who

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:16.920
is playing a black sergeant in Vietnam is
always at odds with Ben Stiller’s lead actor

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.950
and they barely talk to each other.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.610
They don’t get along.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.530
But in the middle part of the movie they start
to actually talk to each other.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.991
It’s the first time that they are actually
trying to make a connection.

00:01:27.991 --> 00:01:37.420
Connection is when things slow down in the
movie where you have heart-to-heart conversations.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.810
You tell the truth about each other.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:46.729
I guess the one thing, the one caveat that
I’d like to put out there is that all these

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:54.490
elements are in all these comedy movies, but
they’re not necessarily in the same order.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:03.060
After connections and new directions and characters
get what we call the Discovered Goal you have

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.170
disconnections which is the dark night of
the soul.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.760
That is usually the part about 3/4 of the
way or 4/5ths of the way through where everything

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:22.989
seems lost and your hero is going to fail
and then somehow they find the power or the

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:25.830
strength one more time and they win.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:31.739
Now normally that comes at about 3/4s of the
way, 4/5ths of the way through the movie.

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:37.030
In GROUNDHOG DAY, however, that dark night
of the soul comes in at about midway through

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.650
it.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:46.870
It happens after the sequence in which Bill
Murray, who because he’s found the skill

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.180
to get whatever he wants by simply being able
to anticipate what’s going to happen because

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:59.680
he’s living the day over and over and over
again, he’s able to rob a truck full of

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.799
money, he’s able to sleep with all the women
in town, and then he gets the idea that he

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:12.099
wants to have a relationship Andie MacDowell
(the producer Rita).

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:18.139
Even though he tried over and over again,
it always ends with this great slap montage…slap,

00:03:18.139 --> 00:03:20.999
slap, slap.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:26.609
And now we’re only about half way through
the movie and that’s the time in which he

00:03:26.609 --> 00:03:29.389
has lost all hope.

00:03:29.389 --> 00:03:35.340
He goes through this great series of suicide
attempts.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.709
They are attempts because he does kill himself
but he wakes up the next day.

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:42.879
In fact, GROUNDHOG DAY is a really interesting
structure.

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:47.439
It’s basically structured after the long
first act in which it sets everything up.

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:54.129
It’s structured like the Five Stages of
Grief of Dr. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross.

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:59.839
There is denial, there is a very short section
of anger, and there’s a long negotiation

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:06.049
period then there is depression, and finally
there is acceptance.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:09.620
But in GROUNDHOG DAY there is three acts to
acceptance.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:17.680
There is getting [the character] Rita to believe
in him, that’s an entire act.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:24.160
Then there is the act of trying to save the
old man in which he’s got to come to grips

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.540
with the fact that he’s not God, he’s
simply a man going through an impossible situation.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.600
And finally there is the final act of acceptance
in which he simply accepts his fate.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.370
He is never going to live to see Feburary
3rd.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.100
He is always going to be living in February
2nd and he’s o.k.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.100
with it.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.990
He’s found out that the purpose of life
for him is simply to do as much good as he

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.780
can in the one day he has.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:05:04.100
So that’s a way in which just understanding
that this framework, this structure isn’t

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.720
a cookie cutter form that must be followed.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.950
That all these parts of the journey happen,
they don’t necessarily happen all in the

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.730
same order or all at the same time.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.500
The thought that certain things have to happen
on page ten and that certain things have to

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:27.680
happen on page twenty-six is probably not
a really good way to approach writing a screenplay.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.680
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS: Can you name another
movie that uses the 5 Stages of Grief by Dr.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:28.686
Elisabeth Kübler-Ross as it’s story structure?

